  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 133 PM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nOnce the 500mb closed low to our northwest finally ejects eastward \nover the north-central Plains, the base of the trough axis should be \ndraped atop the southern High Plains through this weekend. A subtle \nsurface boundary is set to stall over the southern & eastern TX \nPanhandle through the day Saturday, most likely somewhere south of \nthe Highway 60 corridor. Moisture along and ahead of this feature \n(60s dew points & 1.0-1.5\" PWATs) combined with highs in the 90s will \nbe in place when a pair of embedded shortwaves ahead of the \ntrough reach the area. This should create an unstable and slightly\nsheared environment, characterized by 1000-2000 J/kg MUCAPE and \n15-25 kt Bulk Shear. As long as the lead shortwave doesn't greatly\ninhibit the environment, the next disturbance hot on its heels \nshould generate scattered thunderstorms along the boundary in the \nafternoon and evening hours Saturday. Any storms could be capable \nof producing large hail, damaging winds, and localized flooding \nfrom heavy rainfall. \n\nHighest confidence in rain is still mostly confined to the far \nsouthern & eastern Texas Panhandle (30-50% POPs) based on current \nexpectations of where the best surface and upper level features \nshould overlap. Northwest areas have the lowest likelihood of rain, \nbut central areas of the Panhandles including Amarillo could still \nhave a brief window for storm development if the boundary hangs \nnorth, or if ingredients are timed more favorably like the NAM suite \nof guidance is suggesting (20-30% POPs). There is still much \npotential for the timing and location of these features to shift \naround and greatly change the forecast. But for now, something along \nthe lines of the scenario provided in this discussion is a mostly \nlikely outcome given current model data. Depending on the timing of \nthe trough axis' arrival along with a stronger cold frontal passage, \nadditional showers and storms could last Saturday night into Sunday \nmorning across primarily the north and eastern Panhandles. Once the \ntrough exits though, subsidence on the back side of the system \nshould bring activity to an end for most of the region through \nSunday night. \n\nLong range model trends are persistent in predicting a pattern \nchange that would provide more rain opportunities throughout the \nweek for the southern High Plains and Panhandles region. Current \nthinking is that another low pressure system to our west and ridging \nto our south will place southwest or zonal flow atop the region \neach day, helping to maintain anomalously high moisture content. \nThis setup is favorable for at least isolated to scattered rain \nchances any day, but more widespread coverage of soaking rain \nwould be expected further west over New Mexico into the western \nPanhandles where better jet dynamics would be situated. We will \nhave to monitor the synoptic evolution to see what areas have \nhigher probabilities, but global ensembles and long range guidance\nare pinging chances for rain totals >1\" with these systems. At \nthe very least, the pattern should provide near average \ntemperatures in the 70s and 80s throughout the work week. \n\nHarrel",
